<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need a Lot of Books! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need a Lot of Books!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Write descriptive book reviews for peers. interactive: Rewrite the end of a story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write descriptive reviews of books read in the library by providing a summary of the content and their opinion (e.g., 'This book is about a boy who discovers a hidden treasure. It is funny and exciting.'). Can rewrite story endings based on library events (e.g., 'And then, they found a treasure in the library.'). Can add new ideas to simple stories related to library themes (e.g., 'Then, the dog found a hidden book.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of different book genres (fiction, non-fiction, adventure, mystery). Students write down three words that describe what they imagine these books are about. For example, for "adventure," they might write "run, find, treasure."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board about a favorite book, for example: "I read *The Magic Treehouse* last year. It was interesting and had a lot of exciting chapters." The teacher underlines 'read' (past simple) and 'interesting' (adjective) and emphasizes quiet intonation for words like 'book' (short /ʊ/) and 'find' (long /aɪ/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write about a book I liked or disliked?" (Liked)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What tense did I use to talk about reading the book: present or past?" (Past)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "In the Library".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short, simple book reviews (e.g., 2-3 sentences each) written by fictional peers, displayed on a large chart or projector. Each review includes a book title, a brief summary, and an opinion, using past simple and target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Review:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"<b>Book Review: The Hidden Map</b><br>I read this book last week. It is about a girl who found a hidden map in the library. She went on an adventure! It was very interesting and I liked the author's story. You should check it out!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand the structure and content of a simple written book review and extract key information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the example reviews and complete short written tasks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of each book review?" (Students write a one-word answer like "adventure" or "mystery" for each review.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match the book title with the main character or key event mentioned in the review by drawing lines on their worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students write T or F for statements like: "The book 'The Hidden Map' was boring." (F)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using past simple tense, modals (can/should), imperatives, and library-related vocabulary (e.g., read, liked, interesting, quiet, borrow, check out, many, a lot, summary, author).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students complete them using the past simple form of verbs or appropriate library vocabulary from a word bank. Example: "I _______ (read) a book about a ________ (librarian) yesterday. It was very ________ (interesting)." Focus on short /ɪ/ in 'pick' and long /aɪ/ in 'find' when they read their sentences quietly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Sentence Creation:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives prompts (e.g., "Describe a book you borrowed," "Give advice about library behavior," "List books you need"). Students write 2-3 sentences for each prompt, ensuring they use target grammar and vocabulary. Example prompt: "Write about a time you visited the library." Student A writes, "Last Saturday, I went to the library. I found many books." Student B checks it for correct past simple and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Writing & Pragmatics):</b> Students swap their completed sentences from the previous activity. They act as "editors" and check their partner's writing for correct grammar (past simple, can/should, imperatives), appropriate vocabulary, and clarity. They can write "Good job!" or "Check verb here." This practices listing information collaboratively and giving feedback. They also check for intonation if their partner reads a sentence like "Can I borrow this book?"</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[12-15 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will individually write a short book review for a peer or rewrite the end of a familiar story, adding library-themed elements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option A (Book Review):</b> Students choose a book they have recently read (or a fictional one they invent using provided titles). They write a 3-4 sentence book review for their peers. The review must include:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The <b>title</b> and <b>author</b> (if applicable).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A short <b>summary</b> of what the book is about (using past simple, e.g., "It was about a boy who *found* a hidden book.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their <b>opinion</b> of the book (using adjectives like 'interesting', 'exciting', 'funny').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A recommendation using "You should..." or "I think you can...".</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option B (Rewrite Story Ending):</b> The teacher provides the beginning or middle of a very short, simple story (e.g., Goldilocks and the Three Bears, or a story introduced in previous lessons). Students rewrite the ending of the story, making sure it happens "in the library" and involves library vocabulary and actions (e.g., 'librarian,' 'shelf,' 'check out,' 'quiet,' 'read'). They should aim for 3-5 sentences and use the past simple tense. For example, "And then, Goldilocks ran to the library. She found a quiet section and read many books until the bears came."</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging the use of previously learned vocabulary (e.g., 'many,' 'all,' 'most,' 'a lot' for books, 'helpful' for a librarian) and grammar structures (past simple, modals). Students can informally discuss vocabulary with peers (sociolinguistic competence).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written book reviews or rewritten story endings with a partner. Each student uses a simple checklist to provide feedback on their partner's writing. The teacher collects the written work for further review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner use the past simple tense correctly? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner use at least 3 library words (e.g., book, library, librarian, shelf)? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the summary clear and does it make sense? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner write their opinion about the book/story? (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts in writing before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found writing about _______ (books/stories) easy/difficult because ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of my writing do I want to make better next time?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting book I learned about today (even if I didn't write about it)?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., peer feedback, controlled practice with past simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students struggling with past simple or vocabulary for the next writing task? (e.g., provide sentence starters, more examples of descriptive adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize and present their book reviews or story endings, potentially turning them into a collaborative library display or sharing them orally, thus integrating writing with speaking and mediation skills.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3 minutes]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>